WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.360
We explored in part one of this topic,

2
00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:09.220
a stitch pattern in the CB1 box is only
a design made of black and white boxes.

3
00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:15.140
To create color work slip tuck or knit any
automatic stitch patterning technique,

4
00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:19.880
the CB-1 box will select the needles,
and this, combined with the position

5
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:25.860
of the needles and the carriage settings,
are what create the stitch patterns.

6
00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:29.920
Now, one of the fun things to do
with the CB-1 box is to combine designs

7
00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:36.180
across the width of your fabric
for unlimited design possibilities.

8
00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:41.820
We can break a stitch pattern up
into small elements and combine them

9
00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:46.340
instead of creating one
large stitch pattern.

10
00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:49.220
We'll use these two little motifs.

11
00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:54.720
By themselves, they aren't very exciting,
but with a little imagination combined,

12
00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:58.500
they can create an interesting
stitch pattern.

13
00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:04.800
Start from the main menu and choose
the positioning program icon.

14
00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:16.120
I'm using the cake carriage for my design,
but remember, this step gives you options.

15
00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.480
We're going to isolate this exact design,

16
00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:23.320
not have it all the way across
the width of the fabric.

17
00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.140
This is where we set up
the combined pattern.

18
00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:31.290
Motif 1 will be the little square.

19
00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.000
It's pattern number 6 for me,

20
00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:37.820
and I only want it repeated one
time horizontally on my knitting.

21
00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:41.570
It will automatically repeat vertically,

22
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:47.060
so if I'd enter in 2 here,
I'd get the square side by side.

23
00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:53.020
The next step is to position the motif
on the needle bed in relation to 0.

24
00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:59.480
Since my motif is six stitches wide,
I'll enter 3 to position it.

25
00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:05.260
Next, we'll set up the left zigzag motif.

26
00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:09.120
Choose two and then enter
the pattern number.

27
00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:11.940
Mine happens to be 901,

28
00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:18.320
and indicate that you only want, again,
just one repeat of that pattern.

29
00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:22.220
We can position that zigzag
anywhere we want.

30
00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:28.480
Since it's six stitches wide, I'm going
to position it at needle number nine.

31
00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.500
You can check your
positioning with the preview.

32
00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:37.300
Use the arrow buttons on the direction key

33
00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:42.600
to move the view and to double
check your positioning.

34
00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:46.460
We'll do the same thing
for the other side of our design.

35
00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:50.100
We'll position the zigzag
on the right side.

36
00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:53.780
Choose motif 3,
enter in the stitch number.

37
00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:56.220
Now it could be a completely
different motif.

38
00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:58.940
I'm just using my zig.

39
00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:02.880
Enter the number of repeats you want.

40
00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:08.000
Position it in relation to 0 on the needle

41
00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:13.400
bed or in relation to the other
motifs that you already entered.

42
00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.200
You get an opportunity to preview

43
00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:25.320
the positioning,
choose end, and you're ready to knit.

